One effect of jet quenching is the high-\(p_{T}\) hadron cross sections in heavy ion collisions are suppressed from those found in elementary collisions. A useful observable to quantify jet quenching is the nuclear modification factor given by,

\[R_{AB}(p_{T})=\frac{\frac{dN_{AB}}{d\eta d^{2}p_{T}}}{T_{AB}\frac{d\sigma_{NN} }{d\eta d^{2}p_{T}}} \tag{1.10}\]

where A and B are our two colliding nuclei and \(T_{AB}=\langle N_{bin}\rangle/\sigma_{inelastic}^{pp}\), where and \(<N_{bin}>\) is the number of binary nucleon-nucleon collisions in A+B. Figure 1.5 shows the nuclear modification factor in central Au+Au collisions and d+Au collisions. The

